{'id': 30, 'parent_id': None, 'pinned': 0, 'author': 'ash-k', 'title': 'Scaling a 9x13 braise down to an 8x8?', 'body': 'I usually make a 9x13 pork and bean braise, but this week I only want the 8x8 version. The liquid ratio feels fine, but the meat timing is harder to guess.', 'created': 1784322944.7731004}
{'id': 31, 'parent_id': 30, 'pinned': 0, 'author': 'devon.r', 'title': '', 'body': 'I scale by weight, not pan size, and keep the liquid a little shy since the smaller pan loses less to evaporation.', 'created': 1784353576.0102}
{'id': 32, 'parent_id': 30, 'pinned': 0, 'author': 'pkrause', 'title': '', 'body': 'For braises I mostly watch doneness rather than the clock, especially when the depth changes.', 'created': 1784462693.9579341}
{'id': 33, 'parent_id': 30, 'pinned': 0, 'author': 'lin-ops', 'title': '', 'body': 'I’ve had better luck reheating leftovers than nailing a tiny batch fresh. Smaller pans cool faster once they’re out, too.', 'created': 1784386769.3428001}
{'id': 34, 'parent_id': None, 'pinned': 0, 'author': 'ash-k', 'title': 'Doubling a braise and the liquid math is weird', 'body': 'I doubled my usual beef stew recipe for a crowd and it came out much soupier than expected. The meat was fine, but the sauce never really thickened up the way it does at the original size. Anyone else notice scaling liquid separately from everything else?', 'created': 1784632317.7853863}
{'id': 35, 'parent_id': 34, 'pinned': 0, 'author': 'devon.r', 'title': '', 'body': 'I usually hold back about a quarter of the stock and add it near the end if it looks dry. Browning in batches matters more than exact ratios for me.', 'created': 1784659995.6126664}
{'id': 36, 'parent_id': None, 'pinned': 0, 'author': 'ash-k', 'title': 'Scaling a 9x13 casserole down to an 8x8 — any rules of thumb?', 'body': "I keep halving recipes but the pan area doesn't halve, so the bake time is always a guess. Do you scale by volume, by area, or just keep the depth the same and adjust time?", 'created': 1785019212.131644}
{'id': 37, 'parent_id': 36, 'pinned': 0, 'author': 'devon.r', 'title': '', 'body': 'I usually match the depth first, then treat the area ratio as a rough multiplier for time. A 9x13 to 8x8 is about 0.55 area, so I start checking around 70% of the original time.', 'created': 1785123942.5019302}
{'id': 38, 'parent_id': 36, 'pinned': 0, 'author': 'pkrause', 'title': '', 'body': 'For custardy things like lasagna I keep the same depth and just accept a shorter bake; for breads and cakes the pan swap changes structure more than time. I note the next attempt in the margin.', 'created': 1785262073.055867}
{'id': 39, 'parent_id': 36, 'pinned': 0, 'author': 'lin-ops', 'title': '', 'body': "If it's a braise or anything covered, pan size matters way less — I just scale the liquid down and let it simmer until it tastes right. Uncovered bakes are where the geometry bites.", 'created': 1785052569.076954}
{'id': 40, 'parent_id': None, 'pinned': 0, 'author': 'oakmoss', 'title': 'first frost watch: basil still hanging on', 'body': 'My weather log says we’re flirting with the first frost this week, but the basil in the south bed still looks fine. I’m debating whether to harvest now or wait one more night and see what the low actually does.', 'created': 1785358288.9226217}
{'id': 41, 'parent_id': 40, 'pinned': 0, 'author': 'kestrel-07', 'title': '', 'body': 'My log shows the same pattern, though the covered row is doing better than the open bed. I’m watching the dew point more than the forecast low.', 'created': 1785469005.61025}
{'id': 42, 'parent_id': None, 'pinned': 0, 'author': 'ash-k', 'title': 'Halving a lasagna recipe without a sad pan', 'body': "I usually make a 9x13 lasagna for four, but it's just two of us this weekend. If I halve everything, do I use an 8x8 and same temp, or is that asking for dry edges? Curious how others think about pan size versus bake time.", 'created': 1785704170.3883054}
{'id': 43, 'parent_id': 42, 'pinned': 0, 'author': 'devon.r', 'title': '', 'body': 'I scale by weight first, then pick the pan that gives me roughly the same depth. Same temp usually works, but I start checking way earlier and go by feel.', 'created': 1785739998.5859456}
{'id': 44, 'parent_id': 42, 'pinned': 0, 'author': 'marta_codes', 'title': '', 'body': "My notes say halving tomato sauce is easy, but noodles and cheese don't always divide cleanly. I'd rather make the full batch and freeze half in portions.", 'created': 1785802966.996329}
{'id': 45, 'parent_id': 42, 'pinned': 0, 'author': 'tkoh', 'title': '', 'body': "I've had better luck with a smaller pan and a slightly lower temp than with a big pan and less fill. The edges still get crispy, just less volcanic.", 'created': 1785781937.7615504}
{'id': 46, 'parent_id': None, 'pinned': 0, 'author': 'tkoh', 'title': 'How do you shelve oversized art books?', 'body': 'Most of my collection is normal hardcovers, but the oversized art books wreck every shelf arrangement I try. They are too tall for the upper cases and too heavy for the floating shelves. What actually works for people here?', 'created': 1786030648.1345928}
{'id': 47, 'parent_id': 46, 'pinned': 0, 'author': 'riverwell', 'title': '', 'body': 'I gave up on strict alpha order for the giant ones and made a low shelf by height. Easier to pull out and less leaning.', 'created': 1786237294.6309388}
{'id': 48, 'parent_id': None, 'pinned': 0, 'author': 'riverwell', 'title': 'Cookbook shelf is out of control', 'body': 'I have cookbooks stacked on the floor because the shelf is full. I use maybe ten regularly, but the rest are sentimental or aspirational. How do you decide what stays visible?', 'created': 1786340115.4313223}
{'id': 49, 'parent_id': 48, 'pinned': 0, 'author': 'sam-builds', 'title': '', 'body': 'I keep a small shelf of current favorites and store the rest in a closet. Twice a year I swap them out, which feels like shopping without spending.', 'created': 1786369138.7164826}

